+# Homepage performance monitoring
+
+`performance-monitor.js` measures navigation links, Order Now, clicks anywhere
+inside a Seasonal Favorites card, and client-side view switches. It runs locally,
+sends no network requests, and does not change link behavior or page appearance.
+
+Read the most recent 100 measurements with
+`window.dailyGrindPerformance.getRecords()`. Each record includes `id`, `kind`,
+`name`, `startTime`, `duration` (milliseconds), and `source`. They also appear as
+`daily-grind:<id>` measures in the browser Performance timeline.
+
+Subscribe to `daily-grind:performance` on `window` for application analytics.
+The event's `detail` contains the record plus `updated`. Upsert by `id`: a browser
+Event Timing entry can replace an earlier frame estimate for the same click.
+No analytics service is configured by default.
+
+## What the timings mean
+
+- `event-timing`: the browser's click timestamp through the next paint, including
+  input delay, handler work, and presentation delay. The browser rounds this
+  duration to 8 ms and only reports events meeting its minimum 16 ms threshold.
+  See the [Event Timing specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/event-timing/).
+- `animation-frame-estimate`: elapsed time through two animation frames. This
+  allows an intervening paint opportunity, but cannot guarantee screen presentation.
+  It covers browsers without Event Timing and clicks below its reporting threshold.
+- Hidden-page work is discarded, so background throttling does not masquerade as
+  slow rendering. Pending estimates are cancelled on page exit.
+
+These are next-update timings, not the completion of CSS animations, image loads,
+or unrelated subsequent asynchronous work. Normal links navigate to another
+document; this monitor cannot measure that destination's paint. A departure may
+produce no sample if it happens before the current page paints.
+
+## Client-side views
+
+Synchronous `history.pushState`, `history.replaceState`, back/forward, and hash
+changes automatically produce frame estimates. History changes alone do not prove
+that a router has committed a new view. For asynchronous updates, wrap the actual
+view switch so its returned promise resolves after DOM changes are committed:
+
+```js
+await window.dailyGrindPerformance.measureViewSwitch('menu', async () => {
+  const menu = await loadMenu();
+  renderMenu(menu); // Await the framework's commit hook here if necessary.
+});
+```
+
+The helper measures from before `update` runs through its completion and the
+following paint opportunity, preserves its return value, and propagates failures
+without recording a successful paint. Independently initiated switches are timed
+separately. Automatic history records and explicit helper records are separate
+measurements. The current homepage has no client-side router or view renderer.
+
+Run the deterministic timing tests with `npm test`. No server or browser is needed.
